{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 5,
  "estimated_minutes": 35,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must understand why the Times' closure of its distribution business could trigger partial withdrawal liability measured by declining shifts even though wages and pension contributions did not decline enough."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "ERISA's contribution base unit and 70-percent contribution-decline test require some learning, particularly the distinction between the unit measuring withdrawal and the percentage used to calculate contributions."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Section 13-I.1's contribution formula must be read with Section 13-K.3's paid-leave treatment, the shift definitions, and the opinion's account of evidence resolving the ambiguity."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must distinguish the court's independent finding that Section 13-I.1 is ambiguous from its deferential affirmance of the arbitrator's factual resolution in favor of shifts."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The shifts-versus-wages ambiguity is readily distinguishable from the opinion's separate disputes over actuarial assumptions, successive-withdrawal credits, and refund interest."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d01 — Understanding why an obligation calculated as 8% of pay can nevertheless have shifts as its contribution base unit requires separating the contribution amount from the withdrawal-triggering measure. The paid-leave provisions and the distinction between finding ambiguity and resolving it through extrinsic evidence add interpretive work.",
  "reason": "Assign this case to a reviewer comfortable learning a limited ERISA framework and distinguishing legal construction from deferential review of arbitral findings. The central dispute is clearly stated: the Fund wanted shifts as the contribution base unit to establish partial withdrawal, while the Times wanted wages to avoid that liability. Preparation should concentrate on Sections 13-I.1 and 13-K.3 and opinion lines 55–68, with enough statutory and factual background to understand the consequences of each reading. The actuarial-rate, credit-calculation, and refund-interest disputes do not need detailed study to recognize the listed defect."
}
